+        <article class="journal-body" id="journal-text">
+          <h3>The Art of Direct-Trade Bean Sourcing</h3>
+          <p>
+            At The Daily Grind, our pursuit of the perfect cup begins long before the beans ever touch our cast-iron roasters. True coffee excellence is rooted in the soil, the microclimate, and the dedication of the farmers who tend every tree. Each harvest season, our sourcing team travels directly to high-altitude growing regions across Colombia, Ethiopia, and Guatemala. Rather than purchasing bulk commodity coffee through impersonal broker networks, we establish multi-year direct-trade relationships with smallholder family farms and cooperative washing stations. By eliminating intermediaries, we ensure our farming partners receive compensation well above fair-trade baseline standards, allowing them to invest in soil rejuvenation, sustainable shade canopies, and living wages for harvest workers.
+          </p>
+          <p>
+            Altitude is the cornerstone of exceptional cup density and flavor clarity. We exclusively source Arabica varieties grown between 1,700 and 2,200 meters above sea level, where cool mountain temperatures extend the maturation cycle of the coffee cherry. This prolonged ripening period allows complex natural sugars and crisp malic and phosphoric acids to develop within the seed. During the height of the harvest, our partner farms employ rigorous selective hand-picking, harvesting only cherries at optimal deep-crimson ripeness with sugar contents between 20% and 22% Brix. Immediately following harvest, density-sorting floatation tanks isolate and discard under-ripe or hollow cherries, guaranteeing that only the most nutrient-packed beans proceed to processing.
+          </p>
+          <p>
+            Processing methods are tailored to elevate each lot's distinctive terroir. For our bright, floral Ethiopian offerings, beans undergo washed processing with mountain spring water and extended patio drying on raised African drying beds for up to 18 days. This method highlights delicate jasmine aromatics, bergamot, and vibrant stone fruit acidity. For select micro-lots in Colombia and Central America, we collaborate on anaerobic and natural fermentations, fermenting whole cherries in sealed stainless tanks under controlled temperatures to develop lush, wine-like fruit notes of blackberry and caramelized brown sugar. Every lot is moisture-tested, hand-sorted for defects, and sealed in GrainPro protective liners before shipment.
+          </p>
+          <p>
+            When green shipments arrive at our roastery, we craft bespoke roast profiles tailored to each harvest's unique moisture and density. We roast in limited 15-kilogram batches, closely monitoring the rate of rise, development time ratios, and the exact moment of first crack. Our goal is never to mask the bean behind heavy smoke, but rather to unlock the natural sweetness, body, and delicate terroir cultivated at origin.
+          </p>
+        </article>
